---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import MadeByKaj from '../../../components/MadeByKaj.astro';
import LiveCodePlayground from '../../../components/live/LiveCodePlayground.astro';
import CodeBlockWithCopy from '../../../components/live/CodeBlockWithCopy.astro';
import addObjectHtml from '../../../live-examples/skykit/add-an-object/index.html?raw';

const basePath = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const installCode = `npm install @found-in-space/skykit@0.2.0-alpha.1 @found-in-space/star-octree-provider@0.2.0-alpha.0 @found-in-space/star-trees@0.2.0-alpha.0 @found-in-space/three-star-field@0.2.0-alpha.0 three`;
const importCode = `import * as THREE from 'three';
import {
  createSkykitViewer,
  createStreamingStarsPlugin,
  createObject3dPlugin,
  createKeyboardNavigationPlugin,
  createSkyGrabPlugin,
  createSkykitAnimationLoop,
} from '@found-in-space/skykit';
import {
  OCTREE_DEFAULT,
  createStarOctreeProviderService,
} from '@found-in-space/star-octree-provider';
import {
  createObserverShellStrategy,
  createSphereVolumeStrategy,
  combineStrategies,
} from '@found-in-space/star-trees';
import { createThreeStarField } from '@found-in-space/three-star-field';`;
---

<Layout
	title="Add your own object to space | Found in Space"
	description="Use SkyKit object plugins to add app-owned world-space and observer-centric visuals to a star viewer."
>
	<SiteHeader section="Learn & build" />

	<main class="page page--lesson">
		<header class="lesson-hero">
			<nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
				<a href={`${basePath}learn-build/`}>Learn &amp; Build</a>
				<span class="breadcrumb__sep" aria-hidden="true">›</span>
				<a href={`${basePath}learn-build/skykit/`}>SkyKit</a>
				<span class="breadcrumb__sep" aria-hidden="true">›</span>
				<span>Add an object</span>
			</nav>
			<p class="eyebrow">Lesson 2</p>
			<h1>Add your own object to space</h1>
			<p class="lede">
				SkyKit is not only a star renderer. It can host objects owned by your app: markers,
				annotation layers, instrument overlays, navigation guides, and animated effects.
			</p>
		</header>

		<article class="lesson-body prose">
			<section>
				<h2>Learning goal</h2>
				<p>
					This example adds a world-space Hyades sphere marker, a world-space radio bubble
					around Sol, and an observer-centric guide ring. World-space objects use the same
					parsec-to-render-unit scale as the viewer. Observer-centric objects stay attached to
					the viewer, which is useful for guide surfaces and sky overlays.
				</p>
			</section>
		</article>

		<LiveCodePlayground
			id="skykit-add-an-object"
			title="SkyKit object layer"
			description="Toggle the app-owned layers, then edit the marker radius, target, or pulse speed."
			initialCode={addObjectHtml}
			mode="html"
			height="34rem"
		/>

		<article class="lesson-body prose">
			<section class="callout panel">
				<p class="panel__eyebrow">Things to try</p>
				<ul>
					<li>Change <code>RADIO_BUBBLE_RADIUS_PC</code>.</li>
					<li>Move the marker by editing <code>HYADES_CENTER_PC</code>.</li>
					<li>Add a second marker with another <code>createObject3dPlugin()</code>.</li>
					<li>Toggle visibility with the checkbox bindings.</li>
					<li>Change the marker pulse speed in the app-owned animation part.</li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<section>
				<h2>What changed in the code</h2>
				<p>
					The star stream is still the same composition from lesson one, but the session strategy
					now combines the observer shell with a sphere around the Hyades so the marker has
					nearby stars to show. The app creates ordinary Three.js objects, places them in render
					units, then hands them to <code>createObject3dPlugin()</code>.
				</p>
				<p>
					The pulse is not a SkyKit built-in. It is an app-owned part registered through a plugin
					function. That is the core pattern: SkyKit coordinates the viewer, while your app owns
					the interaction and visual behavior that make the experience specific.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="source-panel panel">
				<p class="panel__eyebrow">Source and next steps</p>
				<h2>Use package examples for larger object layers.</h2>
				<p>
					This live example is intentionally small. The SkyKit custom object example shows the
					same Object3D pattern with constellation art, a navigation sphere, and a Hyades
					annotation.
				</p>
				<ul>
					<li><a href="https://github.com/Found-in-Space/skykit" rel="noopener noreferrer">SkyKit repository</a></li>
					<li><a href="https://github.com/Found-in-Space/skykit/tree/main/packages/skykit/examples/custom-object-layer" rel="noopener noreferrer">Custom object layer example</a></li>
					<li><a href={`${basePath}learn/topic/radio-bubble/`}>Found in Space radio bubble lesson</a></li>
					<li><a href={`${basePath}learn-build/skykit/query-stars-without-three/`}>Next: query stars without Three.js</a></li>
				</ul>
			</section>

			<section class="local-panel panel">
				<h2>Use this locally</h2>
				<p>
					In a local app, install the packages you import directly. The object itself is
					ordinary Three.js, so your app can decide how to generate, animate, and dispose it.
				</p>
				<CodeBlockWithCopy code={installCode} label="Install" language="bash" />
				<CodeBlockWithCopy code={importCode} label="Local imports" language="js" />
			</section>
		</article>

		<MadeByKaj />
	</main>
</Layout>

<style>
	.page--lesson {
		max-width: 76rem;
	}

	.lesson-hero {
		max-width: 60rem;
		padding: 3.5rem 0 1.5rem;
	}

	.lesson-hero h1 {
		margin: 0;
		font-size: 4rem;
		font-weight: 600;
		line-height: 1;
		letter-spacing: 0;
	}

	.lesson-body {
		max-width: 54rem;
	}

	.source-panel {
		margin: 2.5rem 0;
	}

	.source-panel h2 {
		margin-top: 0;
	}

	.local-panel {
		margin: 2.5rem 0;
	}

	.local-panel h2 {
		margin-top: 0;
	}

	@media (max-width: 720px) {
		.lesson-hero h1 {
			font-size: 2.75rem;
		}
	}
</style>
